      "title": "Mcap of 4 of top-10 most valued firms erodes ₹87,960 cr, Airtel worst hit",
      "link": "https://www.business-standard.com/markets/news/mcap-of-4-of-top-10-most-valued-firms-erodes-87-960-cr-airtel-worst-hit-126082300193_1.html",
      "description": "The combined market valuation of four of the top-10 most valued firms eroded by Rs 87,960.29 crore last week, with Bharti Airtel taking the biggest hit, in-tandem with a bearish trend in equities.\nLast week, the BSE benchmark Sensex declined 468.42 points, or 0.60 per cent, and the NSE Nifty dipped 114 points, or 0.46 per cent.\n\"Indian equity markets ended the week on a cautious note, extending their recent corrective phase as elevated crude oil prices, rising global bond yields and persistent geopolitical uncertainty weighed on investor sentiment,\" Ajit Mishra  SVP, Research, Religare Broking Ltd, said.\nThe valuation of Bharti Airtel dropped Rs 28,052.96 crore to Rs 12,14,963.15 crore, the most among the top-10 firms.\nTata Consultancy Services (TCS) faced an erosion of Rs 22,070.34 crore from its valuation, which stood at Rs 8,31,436.51 crore.\nThe market capitalisation (mcap) of the State Bank of India declined Rs 20,861.2 crore to Rs 9,64,968.76 crore and that of Hindustan Unile",
      "source_name": "Business Standard Latest",
      "published": "2026-08-23T06:32:15"
    },
    {
      "title": "FPIs add ₹23,544 cr to Indian equities in Aug on earnings, rupee stability",
      "link": "https://www.business-standard.com/markets/news/fpis-add-23-544-cr-to-indian-equities-in-aug-on-earnings-rupee-stability-126082300129_1.html",
      "description": "Foreign Portfolio Investors (FPIs) have accelerated buying in Indian equities this month, infusing Rs 23,544 crore so far in August as improving quarterly earnings, a stable rupee and better market prospects lift sentiment.\nThe inflow comes after FPIs invested Rs 20,200 crore in July, marking a sharp turnaround from four consecutive months of heavy selling and signalling renewed confidence in Indian equities.\nFPIs had withdrawn Rs 49,340 crore in June, Rs 32,963 crore in May, Rs 60,847 crore in April and a massive Rs 1.17 lakh crore in March. Prior to this selling streak, they had invested Rs 22,615 crore in February, according to CDSL data.\nDespite the recent buying, foreign investors have remained net sellers in Indian equities in 2026, withdrawing around Rs 2.3 lakh crore so far -- exceeding the Rs 1.66 lakh crore outflow registered during the entire 2025.\n\"The factors that are driving the FPIs back to the Indian market are: earnings growth revival as reflected in Q1 results, FP",
      "source_name": "Business Standard Latest",
      "published": "2026-08-23T05:06:47"
    }
